course: dictionary
lesson: 15134
title: Scalloped
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Scalloped is an adjective, a describing word. It tells you that something has an edge cut into a row of curves, like the edge of a scallop shell. «The cake had a scalloped edge, with a row of neat little curves all the way round.» The curves are the clue: they show the shape of the edge.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «Ella sewed a scalloped trim on the hem of her dress», the words sewed and trim tell you this is about decoration on cloth. A scalloped edge is a pattern, so people use it to make things look neat and pretty.
explain: In cooking, the word has a different meaning. «Mum made scalloped potatoes for dinner, baked in a creamy sauce.» Here scalloped is not about the shape of an edge. It is the name of a dish made with sliced potatoes baked in sauce. This use is mostly found in the USA.
fact: Scalloped means having an edge cut into a row of curves.
fact: The word is named after the scallop, a sea shell whose edge looks like a row of curves.
fact: «Scalloped» is often used in art, sewing and design to describe a neat, decorative edge.
fact: In cooking, scalloped potatoes are sliced and baked in a creamy sauce (this use is mostly American).
fact: The same word can have a different meaning in a different subject: check the words around it to see which meaning fits.
exercise: «The plate had a scalloped edge.» What does scalloped mean here? | Broken into small pieces | *Having an edge cut into a row of curves | Covered in sauce | Flat and smooth all the way round
exercise: Mum says, «We are having scalloped potatoes tonight.» What are scalloped potatoes? | Potatoes cut into straight chips and fried | Potatoes boiled whole in plain water | *Potatoes sliced and baked in a creamy sauce | Raw potatoes eaten crunchy
exercise: Which word means having a row of curves along the edge? | straight | *scalloped | sharp | smooth
test: «Ella sewed a scalloped trim on her dress.» Where would you most likely find a scalloped trim? | In a bowl of soup | *On the edge of a dress or a cushion | On the goal of a football pitch | Inside a fridge
test: In a recipe book, what does scalloped usually mean? | A row of shells found on the beach | A type of cold rain | A very fast car | *A dish of sliced potatoes baked in a creamy sauce
exam: «The scalloped edge of the cake looked like a row of little waves.» Which meaning fits scalloped best here? | *Having an edge cut into a row of curves | Made of metal | Moving very fast | Very hot
